<div class="learn-box">
**What you will learn**

Separate an ATC-class definition from an ingredient study, define new users,
and measure covered days, persistence and discontinuation.
</div>

**You need:** [the installed packages and practice resources](book-00-start.html#before-you-start),
[concept discovery](book-04-vocabulary.html), and the preceding tutorial chapters.
Run these sections in order in a fresh `book-work` session. All observations are
public synthetic teaching data; the numerical results are not clinical evidence.

## Research question and vocabulary feasibility

For a prespecified ATC class, what fraction of the first treatment year is covered,
and when does the first treatment era end? First establish that the vocabulary
supports that class. The small GiBleed vocabulary has no ATC entries; an ATC-class
estimate is therefore unavailable on this practice resource. The executable
worked ATC analysis therefore uses a separate, explicitly synthetic catalogue
and longitudinal supply histories. A subsequent **celecoxib ingredient** example
shows why the unmodified GiBleed records cannot support an adherence conclusion.

```{r chapter-setup}
library(dsOMOPClient)
source("tools/book/display.R")
source("tools/book/connection.R")
source("tools/book/practice.R")
source("tools/book/case-helpers.R")
practice <- book_connect("gibleed")
conns <- practice$conns
atc <- ds.omop.concept.search(NULL, vocabulary = "ATC", standard_only = FALSE, conns = conns)$pooled
print(data.frame(ATC_classes_available = NROW(atc)))
```

On an ATC-enabled resource, search the intended class with `standard_only = FALSE`,
inspect its class level and descendants, check prevalence, then save that set.
Do not infer an ATC hierarchy from a drug's spelling.

## Run the ATC-class selection on the teaching catalogue

The category chapter's synthetic catalogue lets us execute the class-selection
workflow without pretending that GiBleed contains an ATC hierarchy. The class,
its descendant medicine and its exposure records are all explicitly synthetic.
Six people follow each of three invented supply patterns, indexed at day zero:

| Pattern | Fill days | Days supplied per fill |
|---|---|---|
| Continuous, with early refills | 0, 100, 220 | 120, 120, 125 |
| Two overlapping fills, then stop | 0, 30 | 60, 60 |
| Short course, then restart | 0, 120 | 30, 30 |

Each has a complete observed year. Supply carried forward from early refills
counts once; a restart after a gap over 30 days starts a new era. These public
patterns let us predict the results before looking at protected summaries.

```{r atc-practice}
ds.omop.disconnect(conns = conns)
DSI::datashield.logout(conns)
source("tools/book/fixture.R")
class_practice <- book_fixture(categories = TRUE, drug_histories = TRUE, symbol = "class_omop")
class_conns <- class_practice$conns
classes <- ds.omop.concept.search(NULL, vocabulary = "ATC", standard_only = FALSE,
  conns = class_conns, symbol = "class_omop")$pooled
knitr::kable(classes[c("concept_name", "concept_class_id", "standard_concept")])
class_id <- classes$concept_id[classes$concept_class_id == "ATC 2nd"]
knitr::kable(ds.omop.concept.descendants(class_id, conns = class_conns, symbol = "class_omop")$pooled)
knitr::kable(ds.omop.concept.prevalence("drug_exposure", scope = "pooled", conns = class_conns, symbol = "class_omop")$pooled)
class_set <- omop_concept_set(class_id, descendants = TRUE)
class_file <- tempfile(fileext = ".rds")
saveRDS(class_set, class_file)
```

A classification concept groups descendants; it is not a standard ingredient
substitution through Maps to. The descendant list establishes the selected
medicine before the protected inventory establishes its use.

```{r atc-utilisation}
class_users <- omop_new_users(class_set, washout_days = 365)
class_users$filters <- c(class_users$filters, list(omop_observed(after = 364)))
class_drug <- omop_event("drug_exposure", class_set)
class_era <- omop_era(class_drug, gap = 30, by = "ingredient", stockpile = TRUE)
class_days <- omop_variable(event = class_era, reducer = omop_reduce("covered_days",
  window = omop_window(0, 364), coverage = "effective_supply"))
class_persistence <- omop_variable(event = class_era,
  reducer = omop_reduce("first_era_days", window = omop_window(0, 364)))
class_study <- omop_study(class_users, outputs = "baseline", baseline = list(
  covered = class_days, persistence = class_persistence,
  discontinued = omop_number_flag(class_persistence, "lt", 365),
  pdc = omop_number_derive(list(days = class_days),
    book_expr(book_op("/", book_col("days"), book_lit(365))), unit = "1")))
class_result <- recipe_execute(class_study, out = c(baseline = "D_class_use"), conns = class_conns, symbol = "class_omop")
class_pdc <- dsBaseClient::ds.mean("D_class_use$pdc", type = "combine", datasources = class_conns)
class_pdc$Global.Mean
```

The expected covered days are 365, 120 and 60. Their mean PDC is
(365 + 120 + 60) / (3 × 365), about 0.498. First-era persistence is 365, 120
and 30 days: the restarted course adds covered days but does not extend the first
era. Two of the three patterns meet the protocol's first-era discontinuation rule.
Recorded supply is not proof of ingestion or permanent cessation.
The class has one ingredient; a protocol with several ingredients must decide
whether switching still counts as persistence on the class.

```{r atc-covered-days}
knitr::kable(book_summary(dsBaseClient::ds.summary("D_class_use$covered",
  datasources = class_conns)))
```

The summary spans 60 to 365 covered days, consistent with the declared patterns.
Keep this connection for the persistence and discontinuation exercises. Next,
inspect the unmodified GiBleed ingredient records and their decisive limitation.

```{r return-to-gibleed}
practice <- book_connect("gibleed")
conns <- practice$conns
```

## Find the executable ingredient set

```{r utilisation-discovery}
hits <- ds.omop.concept.search("celecoxib", domain = "Drug", vocabulary = "RxNorm", conns = conns)$pooled
knitr::kable(hits[c("concept_name", "standard_concept", "concept_class_id")])
ingredient_id <- hits$concept_id[trimws(hits$concept_name) == "celecoxib"]
knitr::kable(ds.omop.concept.descendants(ingredient_id, conns = conns)$pooled["concept_name"])
knitr::kable(ds.omop.concept.prevalence("drug_exposure", top_n = 10, scope = "pooled", conns = conns)$pooled)
mapping <- ds.omop.concept.relationships(ingredient_id, relationship_id = "Maps to",
  conns = conns, symbol = "omop")$pooled
children <- ds.omop.concept.descendants(ingredient_id, include_self = FALSE,
  conns = conns, symbol = "omop")$pooled
print(data.frame(Maps_to_rows = NROW(mapping), Descendant_rows = NROW(children)))
drug_set <- omop_concept_set(ingredient_id, descendants = TRUE)
```

## Define new users and the denominator

Require a 365-day washout and a complete next year of observation. This complete-
follow-up restriction simplifies the demonstration but can select healthier or
more stable patients. A real protocol may instead use each person's observed time.

```{r utilisation-population}
users <- omop_new_users(drug_set, washout_days = 365)
users$filters <- c(users$filters, list(omop_observed(after = 364)))
drug <- omop_event("drug_exposure", drug_set)
era <- omop_era(drug, gap = 30, by = "ingredient", stockpile = TRUE)
covered <- omop_variable(event = era, reducer = omop_reduce("covered_days",
  window = omop_window(0, 364), coverage = "effective_supply"))
persistence <- omop_variable(event = era, reducer = omop_reduce("first_era_days",
  window = omop_window(0, 364)))
utilisation <- omop_study(users, outputs = "baseline", baseline = list(
  covered = covered, persistence = persistence,
  pdc = omop_number_derive(list(covered = covered),
    book_expr(book_op("/", book_col("covered"), book_lit(365))), unit = "1"),
  discontinued = omop_number_flag(persistence, "lt", 365),
  fills = omop_variable(event = drug, reducer = omop_reduce("count", window = omop_window(0, 364)))))
book_symbols(recipe_execute(utilisation, out = c(baseline = "D_utilisation"), conns = conns))
knitr::kable(book_summary(dsBaseClient::ds.summary("D_utilisation$covered", datasources = conns)))
```

Every released GiBleed covered-days summary is one day. The public input has
one celecoxib exposure per person, zero `days_supply`, and equal start/end dates:
only the inclusive one-day recorded span is available. It cannot establish
longitudinal supply, adherence, restarts or actual treatment discontinuation.

The stockpiled era carries overlapping supply forward when that information exists,
as in the synthetic ATC example.
Covered days count the union of effective supply days, avoiding double counting
overlap. Era persistence tolerates a declared 30-day gap; it is not the same as
covered days. Recorded prescribing is not proof of actual ingestion.

## PDC and discontinuation

Proportion of days covered (PDC) divides covered days by the 365 eligible days.
The native expression declares a dimensionless result (`unit = "1"`), rather
than retaining the covered-days coordinate after a display rescaling.
A first era shorter than 365 days is a protocol-defined discontinuation indicator
under this gap rule, not proof of permanent treatment cessation.

```{r utilisation-pdc}
book_symbols(recipe_execute(utilisation, out = c(baseline = "D_utilisation_analysis"), conns = conns))
knitr::kable(book_summary(dsBaseClient::ds.summary("D_utilisation_analysis$pdc", datasources = conns)))
```

Here every GiBleed PDC is 1/365 (about 0.00274), solely because of the one-day
recording pattern. This is arithmetic on the available record, not a clinical
adherence estimate. The synthetic ATC example supplies the longitudinal teaching
comparison.

Inspect the definition before interpreting these values. Changing stockpiling,
gap tolerance or the follow-up denominator changes the question even when the
column name remains PDC. Privacy checks still apply to the final table and releases.

## Exercises

**1.** Summarise persistence in both resources. Compare its units with PDC and
explain why only the synthetic example demonstrates varied supply histories.

<details><summary>Solution 1</summary>

```{r utilisation-persistence}
knitr::kable(book_summary(dsBaseClient::ds.summary("D_utilisation_analysis$persistence", datasources = conns)))
knitr::kable(book_summary(dsBaseClient::ds.summary("D_class_use$persistence", datasources = class_conns)))
```

GiBleed persistence is uniformly one recorded day. Synthetic first-era persistence
ranges from 30 to 365 days, with a mean of (365 + 120 + 30)/3 = 171.67 days.
Persistence is measured in days; PDC is a fraction. A tolerated gap contributes
to an era's span but need not contribute covered supply days.

</details>

**2.** Count the discontinuation indicator in both resources and interpret it.

<details><summary>Solution 2</summary>

```{r utilisation-discontinuation}
knitr::kable(book_counts(dsBaseClient::ds.table("D_utilisation_analysis$discontinued", datasources = conns)))
knitr::kable(book_counts(dsBaseClient::ds.table("D_class_use$discontinued", datasources = class_conns)))
```

All 1,745 GiBleed people have an indicator of one because the available record
ends on its start date; this does not establish clinical discontinuation. The
synthetic example has 12 indicators of one and six of zero, as predicted from
the supply patterns. The indicator refers to the first era; restarting remains
possible and is explicitly present in the third synthetic pattern.

</details>

**3.** Save the ingredient definition. Would changing its name to an ATC code
turn this into an ATC-class analysis?

<details><summary>Solution 3</summary>

```{r utilisation-save}
set_file <- tempfile(fileext = ".rds")
saveRDS(drug_set, set_file)
identical(readRDS(set_file), drug_set)
```

No. Class membership comes from the vocabulary hierarchy, not the local label.

</details>

```{r utilisation-cleanup}
ds.omop.disconnect(conns = class_conns, symbol = "class_omop")
DSI::datashield.logout(class_conns)
unlink(class_practice$path)
```

## Recap

Check vocabulary and supply-history feasibility first. The synthetic ATC example
shows continuous supply, overlap and restarting. GiBleed supports ingredient
selection but its one-day records cannot establish adherence. Distinguish
covered supply, era persistence, PDC and protocol-defined discontinuation, and
state the denominator and follow-up selection explicitly.

**Next:** [23. Case study: a harmonised Table 1](book-23-case-table1.html).
